\date{2026-09-04}
\title{The duality axiom}
\author{ulrikbuchholtz}
\taxon{Module}
\import{stt-macros}

\p{We state the duality axiom for the interval: every [finitely
presented](ub-001N) #{\II}-algebra is [quasicoherent](stt-00TA), that is, it is
the algebra of functions on its own spectrum (\ref{ub-001P}). It is one of the
key axioms of both simplicial and triangulated type theory.}

\p{Much of what it gives needs only its simplest instance, the
quasicoherence of #{\II[x]}, which is [SQCP](stt-00TC) and implies
[Phoa's principle](stt-00TC) and the [directedness of #{\II}](stt-00TV)
(\ref{ub-001S}). Following \citek{SY25}, adjoining a variable preserves both
the generalised Phoa principle (\ref{ub-001X}) and quasicoherence
(\ref{ub-0020}), so from SQCP alone every free algebra on finitely many
generators is quasicoherent (\ref{ub-0021}). That gives the #{n}-variable Phoa
principle (\ref{ub-001W}) and the sobriety of the cubes (\ref{ub-001Z}).}

\p{The full axiom is used for algebras with relations. For one relation
#{s = t} it is a Nullstellensatz (\ref{ub-003X}), and from it the interval is
[simplicial](stt-00AC) (\ref{ub-003Y}), hence so are all cubes and simplices
(\ref{ub-003Z}).}

\agda{
open import Foundations.Universes
open import Algebra.Lattice
open import Algebra.Lattice.Instances.Polynomial

module Synthetic.Duality
  {𝓘}
  (Δ¹ : Type 𝓘)
  (I : Lattice Δ¹)
  (I-distr : is-distributive I)
  (let I[x] = Free-Algebra I I-distr)
  where

open Lattice I renaming (0l to i0; 1l to i1)

open import Axioms.UF
}

\agda-imports{
\agda{
open import Foundations.Prelude
open import Foundations.Fin

open import Modalities.Instances.Truncation

open import Algebra.Lattice.Instances.Free.Multivariate I I-distr
open import Algebra.Lattice.Presentations I I-distr

open import Core.SetQuotients
open import Core.CanonicalPushouts
open import Core.Joins
open import Core.SurjectiveMaps
open import Foundations.InjectiveMaps

open import Algebra.Lattice.Instances.Quotient

open import Synthetic.Directedness Δ¹ I
open import Synthetic.Quasicoherence Δ¹ I I-distr
open import Synthetic.Simplicial Δ¹ I
open import Synthetic.Cubes Δ¹ i0 i1
open import Algebra.Lattice.Instances.Free.Inductive Δ¹ I using (Δ^_; Δ-suc; Δ⁰-is-singleton)
}
}

\subtree[ub-001P]{
\taxon{Definition}
\title{Duality}

\p{[Quasicoherence](stt-00TA) of an algebra #{A} says that the counit #{A \to
\Obs(\Spec(A))} is an equivalence: elements of #{A} are precisely functions on
the spectrum. The axiom asserts this for every algebra presented by finitely
many generators and relations.}

\p{This is Axiom 10 of \citek{GWB26}. It is due to \citet{Thm. 4.11}{Ble23},
and reached triangulated type theory by way of the analogous axiom of
\citek{CCH23} in synthetic algebraic geometry. Like the interval itself it is
a hypothesis here rather than a postulate, to be passed to the modules that
want it — as [distributivity](stt-00A3) already is.}

\agda{
duality : Type (lsuc 𝓘)
duality
  = {X : Type 𝓘} (A : Algebra I X)
  → is-finitely-presented A → is-quasicoherent A

duality-is-prop : is-prop duality
duality-is-prop
  = Πᵢ-is-prop (Π-is-prop λ A → Π-is-prop λ _ → is-equiv-is-prop)
}
}

\subtree[ub-001S]{
\taxon{Theorem}
\title{Duality implies quasicoherence of #{\II[x]}}

\p{The free algebra on one generator is presented by one generator and no
relations, so duality applies to it. That instance is [SQCP](stt-00TC), from
which [Phoa's principle](stt-00TC) and the [directedness of #{\II}](stt-00TV)
follow.}

\agda{
I[x]←Polynomial-Algebra-1 : LatticeAlgHom (Polynomial-Algebra 1) I[x]
I[x]←Polynomial-Algebra-1
  = Algebra-id-hom ~refl (initial-lattice-algebra _)

I[x]-is-finitely-presented : is-finitely-presented I[x]
I[x]-is-finitely-presented
  = is-finitely-presented←equiv
      I[x]←Polynomial-Algebra-1 id-is-equiv
      (Polynomial-Algebra-is-finitely-presented 1)

SQCP←duality : duality → SQCP
SQCP←duality d = d I[x] I[x]-is-finitely-presented

Phoa's-Principle←duality : duality → Phoa's-Principle
Phoa's-Principle←duality = Phoa's-Principle←SQCP ∘ SQCP←duality

Δ¹-is-directed←duality : duality → Δ¹-is-directed
Δ¹-is-directed←duality = Δ¹-is-directed←SQPC ∘ SQCP←duality
}
}


\subtree[ub-001X]{
\taxon{Theorem}
\title{Adjoining a variable preserves the generalised Phoa principle}

\p{An algebra #{A} satisfies the [generalised Phoa principle](stt-00TC) when
every arrow #{\alpha : \II \to A} is linear in the sense that #{\alpha(i) =
\alpha(0) \lor (\alpha(1) \land i)}. This property passes to #{A[x]}: the
lattice operations and the structure map of #{A[x]} are those of #{A} on each of
the two coefficients, so an arrow in #{A[x]} is a pair of arrows in #{A} and
linearity is checked coefficientwise. Hence ordinary [Phoa's
principle](stt-00TC) alone gives it for every [free algebra on finitely many
generators](ub-001K). This is \citet{Thm. 7.2}{SY25}.}

\agda{
generalised-phoa-polynomial
  : ∀ {𝓥} {X : Type 𝓥} (A : Algebra I X) (Ad : is-distributive-algebra A)
  → Phoa's-Generalised-Principle A
  → Phoa's-Generalised-Principle
      (Algebra-res A (Free-Algebra (Algebra.str-Lattice A) Ad))
generalised-phoa-polynomial A Ad ph α x
  = Δ²-ext (ph (Δ².s ∘ α) x , ph (Δ².t ∘ α) x)

Polynomial-Algebra-generalised-phoa
  : Phoa's-Principle
    → (n : ℕ) → Phoa's-Generalised-Principle (Polynomial-Algebra n)
Polynomial-Algebra-generalised-phoa ph zero = ph
Polynomial-Algebra-generalised-phoa ph (suc n)
  = generalised-phoa-polynomial
      (Polynomial-Algebra n) (Polynomial-distr n)
      (Polynomial-Algebra-generalised-phoa ph n)
}
}

\subtree[ub-0020]{
\taxon{Theorem}
\title{Adjoining a variable preserves quasicoherence}

\p{If #{A} is quasicoherent and satisfies the generalised Phoa principle, so is
#{A[x]}. This is \citet{Thm. 7.3}{SY25}, stated here for the free algebras
#{L[x_1, \ldots, x_n]}, which is where it is used.}

\p{The spectrum of #{A[x]} is #{\II \times \Spec(A)} — that is the
[restriction equivalence](ub-001U), read at the [initial
algebra](stt-00A0) — so a function on #{\Spec(A[x])} is a #{\II}-indexed
family of functions on #{\Spec(A)}, and quasicoherence of #{A} turns those into
a path #{\II \to A}. Under that identification the counit at #{A[x]} becomes
the map sending a polynomial to the path it traces out,}

##{(a_1, a_0) \longmapsto \lambda i.\, a_0 \lor (a_1 \land i),}

\p{that is, the triangle}

\quiver{
\begin{tikzcd}
	{A[x]} && {\Obs(\Spec(A[x]))} \\
	\\
	&& {(\II \to A)}
	\arrow["{\mathrm{eval}}", from=1-1, to=1-3]
	\arrow["\simeq", from=1-3, to=3-3]
	\arrow["{\mathrm{linear}}"', from=1-1, to=3-3]
\end{tikzcd}
}

\p{commutes, with the vertical map built from quasicoherence of #{A}. The
generalised Phoa principle for #{A} says precisely that the diagonal is an
equivalence: a path is linear, and its two coefficients are its endpoints. So
the counit is an equivalence by three-for-two.}

\agda{
module _ (n : ℕ) where

  private
    module Pn = Algebra (Polynomial-Algebra n)
    open is-lattice-hom

    adj : Δ¹ × Spec (Polynomial-Algebra n)
        → Spec (Polynomial-Algebra (suc n))
    adj = Polynomial-adjoin (initial-Algebra I) I-distr n

    linear : Polynomials (suc n) → (Δ¹ → Polynomials n)
    linear p i = Δ².t p Pn.∨ (Δ².s p Pn.∧ Pn.ι i)

    linear-is-equiv
      : Phoa's-Generalised-Principle (Polynomial-Algebra n) → is-equiv linear
    linear-is-equiv ph = is-equiv←qinv (endpoints , η , ε)
      where
        mono : (α : Δ¹ → Polynomials n) → α i0 Pn.≤ α i1
        mono α
          = Pn.≤←∨
              (sym (ph α i1
                   ∙ ap (α i0 Pn.∨_)
                       (ap (α i1 Pn.∧_) (Pn.ι-alg .pres-1) ∙ Pn.1-top)))

        endpoints : (Δ¹ → Polynomials n) → Polynomials (suc n)
        endpoints α = (α i1 , α i0) ⦃ mono α ⦄

        η : (p : Polynomials (suc n)) → endpoints (linear p) ＝ p
        η p
          = Δ²-ext
              ( ap (Δ².t p Pn.∨_)
                  (ap (Δ².s p Pn.∧_) (Pn.ι-alg .pres-1) ∙ Pn.1-top)
                ∙ Pn.∨-comm ∙ Pn.≤-max' (Δ².mono p)
              , ap (Δ².t p Pn.∨_)
                  (ap (Δ².s p Pn.∧_) (Pn.ι-alg .pres-0) ∙ Pn.0-init')
                ∙ Pn.∨-comm ∙ Pn.0-bottom')

        ε : (α : Δ¹ → Polynomials n) → linear (endpoints α) ＝ α
        ε α = funext→ λ i → sym (ph α i)

    key : (p : Polynomials (suc n)) (i : Δ¹)
        → (λ g → eval (Polynomial-Algebra (suc n)) p (adj (i , g)))
          ＝ eval (Polynomial-Algebra n) (linear p i)
    key p i
      = funext→ λ g →
          let gh = un-is-lattice-alg-hom (g .snd) in
          sym ( gh .pres-∨
              ∙ ap (g .fst .fst (Δ².t p) ∨_)
                  (gh .pres-∧ ∙ ap (g .fst .fst (Δ².s p) ∧_) (g .fst .snd i))
              ∙ ∨-comm)

  Polynomial-Algebra-is-quasicoherent-suc
    : is-quasicoherent (Polynomial-Algebra n)
    → Phoa's-Generalised-Principle (Polynomial-Algebra n)
    → is-quasicoherent (Polynomial-Algebra (suc n))
  Polynomial-Algebra-is-quasicoherent-suc qc ph
    = 3-for-2~ compare Φ-is-equiv (linear-is-equiv ph)
    where
      Φ : Obs (Spec (Polynomial-Algebra (suc n))) → (Δ¹ → Polynomials n)
      Φ = (is-equiv.bwd qc ∘_) ∘ curry ∘ (_∘ adj)

      Φ-is-equiv : is-equiv Φ
      Φ-is-equiv
        = is-equiv-∘ (postcomp-equiv (is-equiv⁻¹ qc))
            (is-equiv-∘ curry-is-equiv
              (precomp-equiv
                (Polynomial-adjoin-is-equiv (initial-Algebra I) I-distr n)))

      compare : linear ~ Φ ∘ eval (Polynomial-Algebra (suc n))
      compare p
        = funext→ λ i →
            sym (ap (is-equiv.bwd qc) (key p i)
                ∙ is-equiv.η qc (linear p i))
}
}

\subtree[ub-0021]{
\taxon{Theorem}
\title{Finitely generated free algebras are quasicoherent}

\p{From SQCP — the quasicoherence of #{\II[x]}, equivalently [Phoa's
principle](stt-00TC) — every #{L[x_1, \ldots, x_n]} is quasicoherent, by
\ref{ub-0020} and \ref{ub-001X} alternating up the tower. This is
\citet{Lem. 7.6}{SY25}, and it is what the rest of this module runs on: the
full [duality axiom](ub-001P) is not needed.}

\agda{
Polynomial-Algebra-is-quasicoherent←SQCP
  : SQCP → (n : ℕ) → is-quasicoherent (Polynomial-Algebra n)
Polynomial-Algebra-is-quasicoherent←SQCP sqcp zero
  = is-quasicoherent←equiv
      (Algebra-id-hom ~refl (initial-lattice-algebra I)) id-is-equiv
      I-is-quasicoherent
Polynomial-Algebra-is-quasicoherent←SQCP sqcp (suc n)
  = Polynomial-Algebra-is-quasicoherent-suc n
      (Polynomial-Algebra-is-quasicoherent←SQCP sqcp n)
      (Polynomial-Algebra-generalised-phoa (Phoa's-Principle←SQCP sqcp) n)

Polynomial-Algebra-is-quasicoherent
  : duality → (n : ℕ) → is-quasicoherent (Polynomial-Algebra n)
Polynomial-Algebra-is-quasicoherent d
  = Polynomial-Algebra-is-quasicoherent←SQCP (SQCP←duality d)
}
}

\subtree[ub-001W]{
\taxon{Corollary}
\title{The generalised Phoa principle}

\p{The spectrum of the [free algebra on #{n} generators](ub-001U) is the
#{n}-cube, since a map of algebras out of a free algebra is a choice of image
for each generator. Quasicoherence of that algebra then reads: a function on
the #{n}-cube is a polynomial in #{n} variables, and the polynomial is
unique.}

##{\II[x_1, \ldots, x_n] \;\xrightarrow{\ \sim\ }\; (\II^n \to \II)}

\p{This is the #{n}-variable form of [Phoa's principle](stt-00TC), the case
#{n = 1} being the ordinary one — which is also all that goes in. Concretely,
an element of #{\II[x_1, \ldots, x_n]} is a family of coefficients indexed by
the #{2^n} vertices of the cube and monotone in the cube's order, so a function
on the cube is determined by, and can be prescribed by, its values at the
vertices. That is the internal counterpart of \em{evaluate at the vertices};
what it does not give is that those values are #{0} or #{1}.}

\agda{
Spec-Polynomial-Algebra
  : (n : ℕ) → Spec (Polynomial-Algebra n) ≃ (Fin n → Δ¹)
Spec-Polynomial-Algebra n
  = mk≃ _ (Polynomial-Algebra-is-free n (initial-Algebra I) I-distr)

generalised-phoa
  : SQCP → (n : ℕ) → Polynomials n ≃ ((Fin n → Δ¹) → Δ¹)
generalised-phoa sqcp n
  = equiv←is-quasicoherent (Polynomial-Algebra-is-quasicoherent←SQCP sqcp n)
  ∙e precomp-≃ (Spec-Polynomial-Algebra n e⁻¹)
}
}

\subtree[ub-001Z]{
\taxon{Corollary}
\title{Cubes are sober}

\p{The #{n}-cube is [sober](ub-001Y): a point of #{\II^n} is the same thing as
a homomorphism #{\II[x_1, \ldots, x_n] \to \II}. Half of this is
unconditional — the cube \em{is} the spectrum of the free algebra, by
[freeness](ub-001U) — and the other half is that the free algebra is
quasicoherent, [the spectrum of a quasicoherent algebra being
sober](ub-001Y). This is \citet{Cor. 7.10}{SY25}.}

\agda{
cube-is-sober : SQCP → (n : ℕ) → is-sober (Fin n → Δ¹)
cube-is-sober sqcp n
  = is-sober←equiv
      (Spec-Polynomial-Algebra n ._≃_.fwd)
      (Spec-Polynomial-Algebra n ._≃_.fwd-is-equiv)
      (Spec-is-sober (Polynomial-Algebra n)
        (Polynomial-Algebra-is-quasicoherent←SQCP sqcp n))
}

\p{Everything above is available under [duality](ub-001P) too, which implies
SQCP by \ref{ub-001S}.}

\agda{
generalised-phoa←duality
  : duality → (n : ℕ) → Polynomials n ≃ ((Fin n → Δ¹) → Δ¹)
generalised-phoa←duality = generalised-phoa ∘ SQCP←duality

cube-is-sober←duality : duality → (n : ℕ) → is-sober (Fin n → Δ¹)
cube-is-sober←duality = cube-is-sober ∘ SQCP←duality
}
}

\subtree[ub-003X]{
\taxon{Lemma}
\title{The Nullstellensatz for one relation}
\date{2026-09-06}

\p{Fix #{s, t : \II} and let #{A = {\II}/(s = t)} be the algebra presented by no
generators and the one relation #{s = t}. Its spectrum is the proposition
#{s = t}: a point is an algebra map #{A \to \II}, that is the identity of
#{\II} together with the fact that it respects the relation. Under
[duality](ub-001P) #{A} is quasicoherent, which reads: a function on the
proposition #{s = t} is merely a constant, and two constants #{a, b} agree on
it only if #{a \sim b} in #{A}. The second half is a Nullstellensatz in
Blechschmidt's sense \citek{Ble23}: from #{s = t \Rightarrow a = b} it
extracts an algebraic certificate, membership in the congruence generated by
#{s = t}, which [the principal congruences](ub-003W) then make explicit.}

\agda{
module Nullstellensatz (s t : Δ¹) where

  presentation : Presentation
  presentation = mk-presentation 0 1 (λ _ → s) (λ _ → t)

  rel : Δ¹ → Δ¹ → Type 𝓘
  rel = presentation-rel presentation

  A : Algebra I (Δ¹ / ⟪⟫-rel {L = I} rel)
  A = Presented presentation

  rel←spec : Spec A → s ＝ t
  rel←spec φ
    = sym (φ .fst .snd s)
    ∙ ap (φ .fst .fst) (glue-quot (Trunc.η (⟪⟫-inc (fzero , refl , refl))))
    ∙ φ .fst .snd t

  spec←rel : s ＝ t → Spec A
  spec←rel e
    = QuotAlgebra-⟪⟫-rec (Polynomial-Algebra 0) rel (initial-Algebra I)
        ( Algebra-id-hom ~refl (initial-lattice-algebra I)
        , λ where x y (_ , px , py) → px ∙ e ∙ sym py )

  module _ (qc : is-quasicoherent A) where
    private
      counit-const : (a : Δ¹) → eval A (ι-quot a) ＝ (λ _ → a)
      counit-const a = funext→ λ φ → φ .fst .snd a

    ⟪⟫←const
      : {a b : Δ¹} → ((φ : Spec A) → a ＝ b) → ⟪⟫-rel {L = I} rel a b
    ⟪⟫←const {a} {b} h
      = unglue-quot (LatticeCongruence.~-equivalence (⟪_⟫ {L = I} rel))
          (unap-equiv qc (counit-const a ∙ funext→ h ∙ sym (counit-const b)))

    const←fun
      : (f : Spec A → Δ¹) → ║ Σ[ a ∶ Δ¹ ] ((φ : Spec A) → f φ ＝ a) ║₋₁
    const←fun f
      = Quot-elim-prop
          {B = λ x → eval A x ＝ f → ║ Σ[ a ∶ Δ¹ ] ((φ : Spec A) → f φ ＝ a) ║₋₁}
          (λ a e →
            Trunc.η (a , λ φ → sym (happly e φ) ∙ happly (counit-const a) φ))
          (is-equiv.bwd qc f) (is-equiv.ε qc f)
}
}

\subtree[ub-003Y]{
\taxon{Theorem}
\title{The interval is simplicial}
\date{2026-09-06}

\p{Under [duality](ub-001P), #{\II} is [simplicial](stt-00AC): for all
#{i, j : \II} the constant map #{\II \to ((i \le j \lor j \le i) \to \II)} is
an equivalence. This is Lemma 3.11 of \citek{GWB26}, which proves it from the
axiom that cubes separate; here nothing but duality is used, and the proof is
algebra. Writing #{P} for the join #{(i \le j) \lor (j \le i)}, a function
#{P \to \II} is a pair of functions on the two disjuncts agreeing on their
conjunction #{i = j}, and each of the three propositions is the spectrum of an
algebra presented by one relation (\ref{ub-003X}). So the statement is that
the square of quotients}

\quiver{
\begin{tikzcd}
	\II && {{\II}/(j \le i)} \\
	\\
	{{\II}/(i \le j)} && {{\II}/(i = j)}
	\arrow[from=1-1, to=1-3]
	\arrow[from=1-1, to=3-1]
	\arrow[from=1-3, to=3-3]
	\arrow[from=3-1, to=3-3]
\end{tikzcd}
}

\p{is a pullback, which holds in every distributive lattice: with the
congruences made explicit by \ref{ub-003W}, injectivity is [the
cancellation law](stt-00A3) at #{w = i \land j}, and surjectivity is witnessed
by #{c = (a \land j) \lor (b \land i) \lor (a \land b)}, which agrees with #{a}
where #{i \le j} and with #{b} where #{j \le i}.}

\agda{
module _ where
  open is-distributive I-distr
  open PrincipalCongruence I-distr

  private
    module N≤ (i j : Δ¹) = Nullstellensatz (i ∧ j) i
    module N= (i j : Δ¹) = Nullstellensatz i j

    bounds≤
      : (i j : Δ¹) {a b : Δ¹}
      → N≤.rel i j a b → bounded-rel (i ∧ j) i a b
    bounds≤ i j (_ , pa , pb)
      = ( ap (_∧ (i ∧ j)) pa ∙ ∧-idem
          ∙ sym (ap (_∧ j) ∧-idem) ∙ sym ∧-assoc ∙ sym (ap (_∧ (i ∧ j)) pb)
        , ap (_∨ i) pa ∙ ∨-comm ∙ ∨-∧-abs ∙ sym ∨-idem ∙ sym (ap (_∨ i) pb) )

    bounds=
      : (i j : Δ¹) {a b : Δ¹}
      → N=.rel i j a b → bounded-rel (i ∧ j) (i ∨ j) a b
    bounds= i j (_ , pa , pb)
      = ( ap (_∧ (i ∧ j)) pa ∙ ∧-assoc ∙ ap (_∧ j) ∧-idem ∙ ∧-comm
          ∙ sym (ap (_∧ i) ∧-idem) ∙ sym ∧-assoc ∙ sym (ap (j ∧_) ∧-comm)
          ∙ sym (ap (_∧ (i ∧ j)) pb)
        , ap (_∨ (i ∨ j)) pa ∙ ∨-assoc ∙ ap (_∨ j) ∨-idem ∙ ∨-comm
          ∙ sym (ap (_∨ i) ∨-idem) ∙ sym ∨-assoc ∙ sym (ap (j ∨_) ∨-comm)
          ∙ sym (ap (_∨ (i ∨ j)) pb) )

    glue-formula
      : (a b i j : Δ¹)
      → a ∧ (i ∧ j) ＝ b ∧ (i ∧ j) → a ∨ (i ∨ j) ＝ b ∨ (i ∨ j)
      → i ≤ j → (a ∧ j) ∨ (b ∧ i) ∨ (a ∧ b) ＝ a
    glue-formula a b i j r₁ r₂ h
      = (a ∧ j) ∨ (b ∧ i) ∨ (a ∧ b)
          ＝⟨ ap (λ x → (a ∧ j) ∨ x ∨ (a ∧ b)) (sym p') ⟩
        (a ∧ j) ∨ (a ∧ i) ∨ (a ∧ b)
          ＝⟨ sym distrib₂-∧-∨ ⟩
        a ∧ (j ∨ i ∨ b)
          ＝⟨ ap (a ∧_) (∨-assoc ∙ ap (_∨ b) (∨-comm ∙ m) ∙ ∨-comm) ⟩
        a ∧ (b ∨ j)
          ＝⟨ ap (a ∧_) (sym q') ⟩
        a ∧ (a ∨ j)
          ＝⟨ ∧-∨-abs ⟩
        a ∎
      where
        m : i ∨ j ＝ j
        m = ≤-max h

        p' : a ∧ i ＝ b ∧ i
        p' = sym (ap (a ∧_) h) ∙ r₁ ∙ ap (b ∧_) h

        q' : a ∨ j ＝ b ∨ j
        q' = sym (ap (a ∨_) m) ∙ r₂ ∙ ap (b ∨_) m

  module _ (d : duality) (i j : Δ¹) where
    private
      P : Type 𝓘
      P = generating-fam-is-simplicial (i , j)

      qc≤ : is-quasicoherent (N≤.A i j)
      qc≤ = d (N≤.A i j) (Presented-is-finitely-presented _)

      qc≥ : is-quasicoherent (N≤.A j i)
      qc≥ = d (N≤.A j i) (Presented-is-finitely-presented _)

      qc= : is-quasicoherent (N=.A i j)
      qc= = d (N=.A i j) (Presented-is-finitely-presented _)

      const-is-injective : is-injective (const: Δ¹ P)
      const-is-injective {a} {b} e
        = cancel-∧-∨ {w = i ∧ j} (cert≤ .fst)
            (distrib-∨-∧ ∙ ap₂ _∧_ (cert≤ .snd) (cert≥ .snd) ∙ sym distrib-∨-∧)
        where
          h : (x : P) → a ＝ b
          h = happly e

          cert≤ : bounded-rel (i ∧ j) i a b
          cert≤
            = ⟪⟫-least (bounded-congruence (i ∧ j) i) (bounds≤ i j)
                (N≤.⟪⟫←const i j qc≤ (λ φ → h (ι₁ (N≤.rel←spec i j φ))))

          cert≥ : bounded-rel (j ∧ i) j a b
          cert≥
            = ⟪⟫-least (bounded-congruence (j ∧ i) j) (bounds≤ j i)
                (N≤.⟪⟫←const j i qc≥ (λ φ → h (ι₂ (N≤.rel←spec j i φ))))

      const-is-surjective : is-surjective (const: Δ¹ P)
      const-is-surjective f
        = Trunc.ind (λ _ → trunc!)
            (λ (a , ha) →
              Trunc.ind (λ _ → trunc!)
                (λ (b , hb) → Trunc.η (witness a b (fix≤ a ha) (fix≥ b hb)))
                (N≤.const←fun j i qc≥ (λ φ → f (ι₂ (N≤.rel←spec j i φ)))))
            (N≤.const←fun i j qc≤ (λ φ → f (ι₁ (N≤.rel←spec i j φ))))
        where
          fix≤
            : (a : Δ¹)
            → ((φ : Spec (N≤.A i j)) → f (ι₁ (N≤.rel←spec i j φ)) ＝ a)
            → (p : i ≤ j) → f (ι₁ p) ＝ a
          fix≤ a ha p
            = ap (λ q → f (ι₁ q)) (carrier-is-set _ _ _ _)
            ∙ ha (N≤.spec←rel i j p)

          fix≥
            : (b : Δ¹)
            → ((φ : Spec (N≤.A j i)) → f (ι₂ (N≤.rel←spec j i φ)) ＝ b)
            → (q : j ≤ i) → f (ι₂ q) ＝ b
          fix≥ b hb q
            = ap (λ q → f (ι₂ q)) (carrier-is-set _ _ _ _)
            ∙ hb (N≤.spec←rel j i q)

          witness
            : (a b : Δ¹)
            → ((p : i ≤ j) → f (ι₁ p) ＝ a) → ((q : j ≤ i) → f (ι₂ q) ＝ b)
            → fibre (const: Δ¹ P) f
          witness a b fa fb
            = ( c
              , funext→ λ x →
                  *-prop-rec (Π-is-prop λ _ → carrier-is-set _ _)
                    (λ p y →
                      left p
                      ∙ ap f (generating-fam-is-simplicial-is-prop (i , j) (ι₁ p) y))
                    (λ q y →
                      right q
                      ∙ ap f (generating-fam-is-simplicial-is-prop (i , j) (ι₂ q) y))
                    x x )
            where
              c : Δ¹
              c = (a ∧ j) ∨ (b ∧ i) ∨ (a ∧ b)

              agree : (φ : Spec (N=.A i j)) → a ＝ b
              agree φ = sym (fa p) ∙ ap f (glue (p , q)) ∙ fb q
                where
                  e : i ＝ j
                  e = N=.rel←spec i j φ

                  p : i ≤ j
                  p = ap (i ∧_) (sym e) ∙ ∧-idem

                  q : j ≤ i
                  q = ap (j ∧_) e ∙ ∧-idem

              cert : bounded-rel (i ∧ j) (i ∨ j) a b
              cert
                = ⟪⟫-least (bounded-congruence (i ∧ j) (i ∨ j)) (bounds= i j)
                    (N=.⟪⟫←const i j qc= agree)

              left : (p : i ≤ j) → c ＝ f (ι₁ p)
              left p = glue-formula a b i j (cert .fst) (cert .snd) p ∙ sym (fa p)

              right : (q : j ≤ i) → c ＝ f (ι₂ q)
              right q
                = rearrange ∙ glue-formula b a j i r₁' r₂' q ∙ sym (fb q)
                where
                  r₁' : b ∧ (j ∧ i) ＝ a ∧ (j ∧ i)
                  r₁' = ap (b ∧_) ∧-comm ∙ sym (cert .fst) ∙ ap (a ∧_) ∧-comm

                  r₂' : b ∨ (j ∨ i) ＝ a ∨ (j ∨ i)
                  r₂' = ap (b ∨_) ∨-comm ∙ sym (cert .snd) ∙ ap (a ∨_) ∨-comm

                  rearrange : c ＝ (b ∧ i) ∨ (a ∧ j) ∨ (b ∧ a)
                  rearrange
                    = ∨-assoc ∙ ap (_∨ (a ∧ b)) ∨-comm ∙ sym ∨-assoc
                    ∙ ap (λ x → (b ∧ i) ∨ (a ∧ j) ∨ x) ∧-comm

    const-is-equiv : is-equiv (const: Δ¹ P)
    const-is-equiv
      = is-equiv←is-surjective-embedding const-is-surjective
          (is-embedding←is-injective (→-is-set carrier-is-set) const-is-injective)

  Δ¹-is-simplicial : duality → is-simplicial Δ¹
  Δ¹-is-simplicial d (i , j) = const-is-equiv d i j
}
}

\subtree[ub-003Z]{
\taxon{Corollary}
\title{Cubes and simplices are simplicial}
\date{2026-09-06}

\p{Simplicial types are closed under finite products, dependent sums and
identity types, and a chain condition #{y \le x} is an identity type in
#{\II}. So every cube and every simplex is simplicial, under duality.
More generally the spectrum of \em{any} #{\II}-algebra is, being a subtype of
a function type into #{\II} cut out by equations in #{\II}; and so is every
convex subtype of a cube, cut out by finitely many inequalities between
coordinates. What is not closed under is joins: the split square
#{\{(i,j) \mid i \le j \lor j \le i\}} is not simplicial, since its reflection
is the square.}

\agda{
□-is-simplicial : duality → (n : ℕ) → is-simplicial (□^ n)
□-is-simplicial d zero = is-simplicial←is-singleton 𝟙ᴸ-is-singleton
□-is-simplicial d (suc zero) = Δ¹-is-simplicial d
□-is-simplicial d (suc (suc n))
  = ×-is-simplicial (□-is-simplicial d (suc n)) (Δ¹-is-simplicial d)

Δ-is-simplicial : duality → (n : ℕ) → is-simplicial (Δ^ n)
Δ-is-simplicial d zero = is-simplicial←is-singleton Δ⁰-is-singleton
Δ-is-simplicial d (suc n)
  = is-simplicial←equiv (equiv←qequiv Δ-suc e⁻¹)
      (Σ-is-simplicial (Δ-is-simplicial d n) λ _ →
        Σ-is-simplicial (Δ¹-is-simplicial d) λ _ →
          ＝-is-simplicial (Δ¹-is-simplicial d))
}
}
